1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,712
This is ECE 358, video 22 a. The topic is multiple access protocols, part 1: TDMA, FDMA and ALOHA.

2
00:00:12,162 --> 00:00:27,501
After this video, you can explain the problem of a shared broadcast channel. You can calculate rates and times on TDMA and FDMA channels. And you can use the ALOHA efficiencies to find the rate of each node.

3
00:00:27,951 --> 00:00:33,220
There are two types of links. A point-to-point link has one sender and one receiver.

4
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:40,305
Examples are the link between a host and an Ethernet switch, and P P P for dial-up access.

5
00:00:40,755 --> 00:00:47,261
A broadcast link is a shared wire or a shared medium. Many nodes connect to the same channel.

6
00:00:47,711 --> 00:00:55,242
Examples are old-fashioned Ethernet on a shared cable, and the upstream channel of a cable access network.

7
00:00:55,692 --> 00:01:01,644
Radio is also a shared medium: WiFi, 4G and 5G, and satellite.

8
00:01:02,094 --> 00:01:08,281
On a broadcast link, every node receives every frame. So the nodes must share the channel.

9
00:01:08,731 --> 00:01:12,357
Here, four hosts, A, B, C and D, share one channel.

10
00:01:12,807 --> 00:01:17,351
When node A sends a frame, the signal reaches every node on the channel.

11
00:01:17,801 --> 00:01:20,532
Now A and C transmit at the same time.

12
00:01:20,982 --> 00:01:27,979
Node B receives two signals at the same time. This is a collision. B cannot use either frame.

13
00:01:28,429 --> 00:01:35,512
A multiple access protocol is a distributed algorithm. It decides when a node can transmit.

14
00:01:35,962 --> 00:01:42,298
The nodes must agree on this through the channel itself. There is no separate channel for coordination.

15
00:01:42,748 --> 00:01:49,724
What does an ideal multiple access protocol do? The channel has a rate of R bits per second.

16
00:01:50,174 --> 00:01:56,318
First property: when only one node wants to transmit, it can send at the full rate R.

17
00:01:56,768 --> 00:02:03,787
Second property: when M nodes want to transmit, each node gets an average rate of R over M.

18
00:02:04,237 --> 00:02:14,605
Here, M is 4. Each frame is still sent at rate R. But node 1 gets 2 of the 8 frames. R times 2 over 8 is R over 4.

19
00:02:15,055 --> 00:02:26,169
Third property: the protocol is fully decentralized. There is no special node that coordinates, and there is no synchronization of clocks or time slots.

20
00:02:26,619 --> 00:02:33,574
Fourth property: it is simple. That is, plug-and-play. The question bank asks for these four properties.

21
00:02:34,024 --> 00:02:39,827
There are three broad classes of MAC protocols. MAC means medium access control.

22
00:02:40,277 --> 00:02:48,447
The first class is channel partitioning. It divides the channel into smaller pieces: time slots or frequency bands.

23
00:02:48,897 --> 00:02:54,359
Each node gets a piece for its exclusive use. Cellular networks use this class.

24
00:02:54,809 --> 00:03:02,254
The second class is random access. The channel is not divided. A node transmits at the full rate R.

25
00:03:02,704 --> 00:03:10,469
So collisions can occur, and the protocol must recover from them. Ethernet on the fixed Internet uses this class.

26
00:03:10,919 --> 00:03:18,386
The third class is taking turns. The nodes take turns, and nodes with more to send can take longer turns.

27
00:03:18,836 --> 00:03:20,671
Bluetooth is an example.

28
00:03:21,121 --> 00:03:29,014
The first partitioning protocol is TDMA, time division multiple access. Time is cut into slots.

29
00:03:29,464 --> 00:03:39,363
The slots are grouped, and each group is a frame. Here each frame has 6 slots. Access to the channel is in rounds: one frame is one round.

30
00:03:39,813 --> 00:03:49,647
Each station gets a fixed slot in each round. Station 1 always uses slot 1, station 2 always uses slot 2, and so on.

31
00:03:50,097 --> 00:03:53,575
The length of a slot is the time to send one packet.

32
00:03:54,025 --> 00:03:58,697
In the slide example, only stations 1, 3 and 4 have packets.

33
00:03:59,147 --> 00:04:07,872
So slots 2, 5 and 6 go idle. No other station can use them. This is the cost of TDMA at low load.

34
00:04:08,322 --> 00:04:17,282
The word frame has two meanings here. It can mean a data frame. In TDMA, it also means a block of consecutive slots.

35
00:04:17,732 --> 00:04:31,172
The second partitioning protocol is FDMA, frequency division multiple access. The frequency spectrum of the channel is divided into frequency bands. Here there are 6 bands.

36
00:04:31,622 --> 00:04:41,670
Again, stations 1, 3 and 4 have packets. Each station gets a fixed frequency band. It can send at any time, but only in its band.

37
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:47,389
The 6 bands share the rate R. So each band carries R divided by 6.

38
00:04:47,839 --> 00:04:52,362
Bands 2, 5 and 6 go idle. No other station can use them.

39
00:04:52,812 --> 00:05:01,153
So FDMA divides frequency, and TDMA divides time. In both, an unused piece is wasted.

40
00:05:01,603 --> 00:05:20,995
Here is question 28 of the link layer question bank. A TDMA channel has a rate of 1 Mbps. A slot is 1 ms, and a frame has 10 slots. 6 nodes are active, and they always have data. There are five parts. Pause the video and try it.

41
00:05:21,445 --> 00:05:28,976
Part a. In one slot, a node sends at the full channel rate, 1 Mbps, for 1 ms.

42
00:05:29,426 --> 00:05:38,343
1 Mbps is 10 to the power 6 bits per second. 1 ms is 10 to the power minus 3 seconds.

43
00:05:38,793 --> 00:05:45,172
10 to the power 6, times 10 to the power minus 3, is 1000 bits per slot.

44
00:05:45,622 --> 00:05:53,025
Part b. The efficiency is the number of slots that carry data, divided by the number of slots in a frame.

45
00:05:53,475 --> 00:06:02,925
6 of the 10 slots carry data. 6 divided by 10 is point 6, or 60 percent. The 4 idle slots are wasted.

46
00:06:03,375 --> 00:06:10,095
Part c. A frame has 10 slots of 1 ms each. So a frame lasts 10 ms.

47
00:06:10,545 --> 00:06:22,087
Each node sends 1000 bits in each frame. 1000 bits per 10 ms, which is point 0 1 seconds, is 100 kbps.

48
00:06:22,537 --> 00:06:35,379
One hour is 3600 seconds. 100 kbps times 3600 seconds is 3 point 6 times 10 to the power 8 bits.

49
00:06:35,829 --> 00:06:42,187
Part d. Inside its own slot, a node sends at the full rate of 1 Mbps.

50
00:06:42,637 --> 00:06:50,061
But over time, its average rate is only 100 kbps, because it gets one slot in ten.

51
00:06:50,511 --> 00:07:04,868
Part e. 1 megabit is 10 to the power 6 bits. Each frame carries 1000 bits of this node. So the node needs 10 to the power 6 divided by 1000, which is 1000 frames.

52
00:07:05,318 --> 00:07:13,489
1000 frames times 10 ms is 10 000 ms, which is about 10 seconds.

53
00:07:13,939 --> 00:07:33,117
Question 29 uses FDMA with the same numbers. The channel rate is 1 Mbps, and it has 10 frequency bands. 6 nodes are active. How many bits can a node send in one second? And how long does 1 megabit take? Pause the video and try it.

54
00:07:33,567 --> 00:07:37,535
The 10 bands share the 1 Mbps rate equally.

55
00:07:37,985 --> 00:07:48,332
So each band carries 1 Mbps divided by 10, which is 100 kbps. A node sends in its band all the time.

56
00:07:48,782 --> 00:07:58,019
In one second, a node sends 100 kbps times 1 second, which is 100 000 bits.

57
00:07:58,469 --> 00:08:12,571
1 megabit is 10 to the power 6 bits, and 100 kbps is 10 to the power 5 bits per second. 10 to the power 6, divided by 10 to the power 5, is 10 seconds.

58
00:08:13,021 --> 00:08:24,114
This is the same as in TDMA. TDMA gives the full rate for one tenth of the time. FDMA gives one tenth of the rate all the time.

59
00:08:24,564 --> 00:08:35,337
Now the second class: random access. When a node has a frame, it transmits at the full channel rate R. There is no coordination before it sends.

60
00:08:35,787 --> 00:08:39,798
If two nodes transmit at the same time, the frames collide.

61
00:08:40,248 --> 00:08:45,048
A receiver gets two signals at the same time, and both frames are lost.

62
00:08:45,498 --> 00:08:51,621
So a random access protocol specifies two things. First, how to detect collisions.

63
00:08:52,071 --> 00:08:57,361
Second, how to recover from collisions, for example with delayed retransmissions.

64
00:08:57,811 --> 00:09:02,270
Each node waits a random delay, so the next tries do not collide.

65
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:15,733
Examples are ALOHA and slotted ALOHA from 1970, CSMA, and CSMA/CD, which Ethernet used from 1973.

66
00:09:16,183 --> 00:09:24,567
WiFi uses CSMA/CA. Modern wired LANs use layer 2 switches, so they have no collisions.

67
00:09:25,017 --> 00:09:37,903
The first random access protocols were the ALOHA protocols, from the University of Hawaii in the 1970s. There are two types: slotted ALOHA and pure ALOHA.

68
00:09:38,353 --> 00:09:48,550
Slotted ALOHA makes these assumptions. All frames have the same size. Time is cut into slots, and one slot is the time to send one frame.

69
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:59,112
Nodes start only at the start of a slot. The nodes are synchronized. And if two or more nodes transmit in a slot, all nodes detect the collision.

70
00:09:59,562 --> 00:10:02,997
Here, both nodes get a fresh frame during slot 1.

71
00:10:03,447 --> 00:10:10,785
Each node waits for the start of the next slot and transmits. Both transmit in slot 2, so there is a collision.

72
00:10:11,235 --> 00:10:16,803
After a collision, each node retransmits in each later slot with probability p.

73
00:10:17,253 --> 00:10:27,600
In slot 3, node 1 waits, with probability 1 minus p. Node 2 transmits. Only one node transmits, so slot 3 is a success.

74
00:10:28,050 --> 00:10:36,477
In slot 4, node 1 transmits and succeeds. When a node has no collision, it can send a new frame in the next slot.

75
00:10:36,927 --> 00:10:45,033
Why is the choice random? If both nodes always retransmitted in the next slot, they would collide again in every slot.

76
00:10:45,483 --> 00:10:53,718
Here is the example from slide 28, with three nodes. C means collision, S means success, and E means empty.

77
00:10:54,168 --> 00:10:58,883
In slot 1, all three nodes transmit, so the slot is a collision.

78
00:10:59,333 --> 00:11:00,933
Slot 2 is empty.

79
00:11:01,383 --> 00:11:04,775
In slot 3, nodes 1 and 2 transmit: collision.

80
00:11:05,225 --> 00:11:08,809
In slot 4, only node 2 transmits: success.

81
00:11:09,259 --> 00:11:10,965
Slot 5 is empty.

82
00:11:11,415 --> 00:11:15,021
In slot 6, nodes 1 and 3 transmit: collision.

83
00:11:15,471 --> 00:11:17,156
Slot 7 is empty.

84
00:11:17,606 --> 00:11:21,126
In slot 8, only node 1 transmits: success.

85
00:11:21,576 --> 00:11:25,181
In slot 9, only node 3 transmits: success.

86
00:11:25,631 --> 00:11:33,610
Count the S slots. Only 3 of the 9 slots carry a frame. The others are wasted by collisions or are empty.

87
00:11:34,060 --> 00:11:44,513
Pros: a single active node can send at the full rate. The protocol is highly decentralized: only the slots must be in sync. And it is simple.

88
00:11:44,963 --> 00:11:51,321
Cons: collisions and idle slots waste the channel, and the nodes need clock synchronization.

89
00:11:51,771 --> 00:12:01,285
The efficiency of slotted ALOHA is the long-run fraction of successful slots, when there are many nodes, all with many frames to send.

90
00:12:01,735 --> 00:12:07,431
For example, suppose 2000 of 10 000 slots are successful.

91
00:12:07,881 --> 00:12:13,791
The efficiency is 2000 divided by 10 000, which is point 2.

92
00:12:14,241 --> 00:12:23,350
The slide gives a derivation, and says you can skip its details. Suppose N nodes, and each sends in a slot with probability p.

93
00:12:23,800 --> 00:12:34,296
One given node succeeds when it sends, and the other N minus 1 nodes do not. That is p times 1 minus p, to the power N minus 1.

94
00:12:34,746 --> 00:12:40,442
Any of the N nodes can be the one that succeeds. So the efficiency is N times that.

95
00:12:40,892 --> 00:12:50,108
The plot shows this for N equals 10. The maximum is at p equals 1 over N, which is 1 over 10, or point 1.

96
00:12:50,558 --> 00:12:57,961
The efficiency there is 10 times point 1 times point 9 to the power 9. That is point 3 8 7.

97
00:12:58,411 --> 00:13:06,475
For N equals 50, the maximum is at p equals point 0 2. The efficiency is point 3 7 2.

98
00:13:06,925 --> 00:13:13,410
As N becomes very large, the maximum goes to 1 over e, which is point 3 7.

99
00:13:13,860 --> 00:13:21,071
Remember this number. At best, slotted ALOHA uses 37 percent of the time for useful frames.

100
00:13:21,521 --> 00:13:29,969
Pure ALOHA is simpler. It has no slots and no synchronization. When a frame arrives, the node transmits it immediately.

101
00:13:30,419 --> 00:13:39,912
Take frame i, which starts at time t zero. The time unit is one frame time, so frame i ends at t zero plus 1.

102
00:13:40,362 --> 00:13:49,877
A frame that started at any time after t zero minus 1 is still on the channel at t zero. It overlaps the start of frame i.

103
00:13:50,327 --> 00:13:56,855
A frame that starts at any time before t zero plus 1 overlaps the end of frame i.

104
00:13:57,305 --> 00:14:07,843
So frame i collides with any frame sent from t zero minus 1 to t zero plus 1. This vulnerable window is 2 frame times long.

105
00:14:08,293 --> 00:14:24,464
In slotted ALOHA, the window is only one slot. So the efficiency of pure ALOHA is half: 1 over 2 e. That is point 3 7 divided by 2, about point 1 8, or 18 percent. Remember this number too.

106
00:14:24,914 --> 00:14:43,346
Slide 43 solves a problem with these numbers. 100 nodes share a medium with slotted ALOHA. The rate of the medium is 10 Mbps. What is the maximum average rate at which one node can successfully send frames? Pause the video and try it.

107
00:14:43,796 --> 00:14:47,785
The blue bar is the channel rate: 10 Mbps.

108
00:14:48,235 --> 00:14:57,153
Treat 100 nodes as a large number. So the maximum efficiency of slotted ALOHA is 1 over e, point 3 7.

109
00:14:57,603 --> 00:15:09,976
Only 37 percent of the time carries successful frames. So the useful rate is point 3 7 times 10 Mbps, which is 3 point 7 Mbps.

110
00:15:10,426 --> 00:15:16,869
The 100 nodes share this useful rate equally. Each node gets one part in 100.

111
00:15:17,319 --> 00:15:29,799
3 point 7 Mbps divided by 100 is 37 000 bits per second, which is 37 kbps. That is the answer.

112
00:15:30,249 --> 00:15:47,955
Questions 37 and 38 of the question bank are the same type. 100 nodes share a 1 Mbps link. Find the average successful rate of one node, first with slotted ALOHA, then with pure ALOHA. Pause the video and try it.

113
00:15:48,405 --> 00:15:54,016
Question 37, slotted ALOHA. The link rate is 1 Mbps.

114
00:15:54,466 --> 00:16:10,893
100 nodes is a large number, so use the maximum efficiency, point 3 7. Point 3 7 times 1 Mbps is point 3 7 Mbps, which is 370 kbps.

115
00:16:11,343 --> 00:16:21,753
Divide by 100 nodes. Each node gets 370 kbps over 100, which is 3 point 7 kbps.

116
00:16:22,203 --> 00:16:33,147
Question 38, pure ALOHA. The efficiency is point 1 8. Point 1 8 times 1 Mbps is 180 kbps.

117
00:16:33,597 --> 00:16:39,229
Divided by 100 nodes, each node gets 1 point 8 kbps.

118
00:16:39,679 --> 00:16:44,095
So slotted ALOHA gives each node twice the rate of pure ALOHA.

119
00:16:44,545 --> 00:16:58,967
With the exact values, 1 over e and 1 over 2 e, the answers are 3 point 6 8 and 1 point 8 4 kbps. The slides use point 3 7 and point 1 8.

120
00:16:59,417 --> 00:17:08,675
Now the descriptive questions of the question bank, from question 20. Read questions 20 to 23. Pause the video and try it.

121
00:17:09,125 --> 00:17:16,165
Question 20. Multiple access means that many nodes share, or access, one broadcast channel.

122
00:17:16,615 --> 00:17:26,194
Question 21. If two nodes start at the same time, there is a collision. Receivers get two signals at once, and both frames are lost.

123
00:17:26,644 --> 00:17:31,572
Question 22. The protocol decides when a node can transmit.

124
00:17:32,022 --> 00:17:44,054
Question 23. The four attributes: one node sends at rate R. M nodes each get R over M on average. It is fully decentralized. And it is simple.

125
00:17:44,504 --> 00:17:52,291
Questions 24 to 27 are about the classes and about channel partitioning. Pause the video and try it.

126
00:17:52,741 --> 00:18:02,469
Question 24. Channel partitioning divides the channel into pieces, such as time slots or frequency bands, for exclusive use.

127
00:18:02,919 --> 00:18:12,071
Random access does not divide the channel. Nodes send at the full rate, collisions occur, and the protocol detects and recovers.

128
00:18:12,521 --> 00:18:18,665
In taking turns, nodes take turns, and nodes with more to send can take longer turns.

129
00:18:19,115 --> 00:18:23,403
Question 25. Partition by time or by frequency.

130
00:18:23,853 --> 00:18:33,517
Question 26. In TDMA, time is slotted, and each node gets a fixed slot in each round. Unused slots go idle.

131
00:18:33,967 --> 00:18:42,137
Question 27. In FDMA, each node gets a fixed frequency band. Unused bands go idle.

132
00:18:42,587 --> 00:18:48,817
Questions 30 to 36 are about random access and ALOHA. Pause the video and try it.

133
00:18:49,267 --> 00:18:58,632
Question 30. A frame collision occurs when two or more nodes transmit at the same time, and a receiver gets both signals at once.

134
00:18:59,082 --> 00:19:06,869
Question 31. A random access protocol specifies how to detect collisions and how to recover from them.

135
00:19:07,319 --> 00:19:14,359
Question 32. In pure ALOHA, a node transmits a new frame immediately. There are no slots.

136
00:19:14,809 --> 00:19:24,643
Question 33. Its max efficiency is 1 over 2 e, which is 18 percent. Its vulnerable window is 2 frame times.

137
00:19:25,093 --> 00:19:35,696
Question 34. In slotted ALOHA, a node sends in the next slot. After a collision, it retransmits in each later slot with probability p.

138
00:19:36,146 --> 00:19:44,594
Question 35. Its max efficiency is 1 over e, 37 percent. Its window is only one slot.

139
00:19:45,044 --> 00:19:56,329
Question 36. Slotted ALOHA is better because frames start only at slot boundaries. So the vulnerable window is one slot, not two frame times.

140
00:19:56,779 --> 00:20:04,225
To recap. A broadcast link needs a multiple access protocol, which decides when a node can transmit.

141
00:20:04,675 --> 00:20:09,859
There are three classes: channel partitioning, random access, and taking turns.

142
00:20:10,309 --> 00:20:17,818
TDMA gives each node a fixed slot, and FDMA a fixed band. Unused pieces go idle.

143
00:20:18,268 --> 00:20:36,977
Slotted ALOHA reaches at most 37 percent, and pure ALOHA 18 percent. For the rate per node, multiply the efficiency by R, and divide by the number of nodes. The next video covers CSMA/CD and the taking turns protocols.
